project: zenmetrics — encoder-search-loop integration cost for zensim / SSIMULACRA2 / butteraugli
task: #163
date_utc: 2026-08-30
question: "what does ONE MORE distorted candidate cost, once the reference is warm,
          and where is the CPU/GPU crossover for a codec quality-search loop"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOST — note this is NOT the 7950X/RTX-5070 box the 2026-05 sweeps used.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
host: Apple M4 Pro, 12 cores, 24 GB unified memory, macOS (Darwin 25.5.0)
gpu_on_this_host: Apple integrated (Metal via wgpu). NO CUDA.
  Metal GPU numbers WERE measured here — driver
  crates/zenmetrics-api/examples/loop_gpu_probe.rs, through the umbrella
  `Metric` API with Backend::Wgpu, 15 reps x 2 runs, min of medians, results in
  metric_loop_gpu_metal_2026-08-30.tsv. Build:
    cargo build --release -p zenmetrics-api --no-default-features \
      --features wgpu,butter,ssim2,zensim,cubecl-types --example loop_gpu_probe
  Run: LOOP_GPU_BACKEND=wgpu LOOP_GPU_SIZES=256,512,1024,2048 LOOP_GPU_REPS=15
  CUDA numbers are NOT re-measured — they are quoted verbatim from the
  previously-committed benchmarks/gpu_coldstart_2026-05-29.tsv (RTX 5070) and
  are labelled as CUDA everywhere they appear. The two GPU datasets are on
  different machines and are never subtracted from each other; each is compared
  only against a CPU baseline, and the Metal one against THIS host's CPU.
  Metal caveats honoured: `fast-reduction` left off for ssim2/butteraugli (Metal
  silently no-ops Atomic<f32>::fetch_add). The known-red Metal tests
  (zensim-gpu cached_ref_slot_rebuild / diffmap_invariants, cvvdp-gpu Mode B
  walker) were not triggered by this probe — it does not use cvvdp, does not
  rebuild an instance at a foreign size, and does not request a CPU diffmap.
  GPU sample discipline: the FIRST metric_new in a process absorbs the cubecl
  device-registry init, so the 256^2 butteraugli `auto` constructor row is
  inflated; per-metric setup is read from the >=512^2 rows.
toolchain: rustc 1.98.0 (88d9e12ae 2026-08-18)
repo_commit (build base): f240bb937fdaabee5e0f7f4d1cdcfd33c49f01e1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARNESS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
driver: benchmarks/heaptrack/drivers/cpu_profile/src/bin/loop_wall.rs  (bin: loop-wall)
sweep:  scripts/loopbench_sweep.sh
fit:    scripts/loopbench_analyze.py
build:  cargo build --release -p cpu-profile --bin loop-wall
        RUSTFLAGS="", NO `-C target-cpu=native` (runtime SIMD dispatch is what
        callers get — CLAUDE.md). Runs under `nice -n 19`.
run:    LOOP_WALL_NO_GATE=1 loop-wall <size> <out.tsv> [metric]

why a new driver instead of reusing cpu-wall:
  cpu-wall (#139/#141) times the packed sRGB u8 entry points. jxl-encoder's
  three search loops do not use them. `vardct/perceptual_loop.rs`,
  `vardct/zensim_loop.rs` and `vardct/ssim2_loop.rs` all hold the
  reconstruction as PLANAR LINEAR f32 at a padded stride (`recon_r/g/b`,
  stride `padded_width`) and score the full frame — there is no u8 anywhere in
  them. loop-wall measures that shape. cpu-wall was extended in the same
  change with the missing 64/128/256 size labels (its ladder started at 512²).

entry points timed (all planar linear f32, stride = width + 16):
  butter  ButteraugliReference::new_linear_planar / compare_linear_planar
          / compare_linear_planar_into (the diffmap-recycling variant)
  zensim  Zensim::precompute_reference_linear_planar
          / compute_with_ref_and_diffmap_linear_planar
  ssim2   Ssimulacra2Reference::new / compare / compare_with(&mut CompareContext)
          — via imgref::Img, since fast-ssim2 has NO planar entry point

cells: oneshot | precompute | warm_strided | warm_zeroalloc |
       warm_tight_plus_copy (zensim) | copy_only | loop_n5
sizes: 64² 128² 256² 512² 1024² 2048² 4096²   (0.004 → 16.8 MP)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# STATISTICS — why MIN across runs, not mean
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
n_runs: 4 single-thread + 4 multi-thread + 2 multi-thread with fast-ssim2 `rayon`
estimator: per-cell MINIMUM across runs.
  This box is shared with other agent sessions and was NOT quiet: a competing
  `sectioned_k_corpus` job held ~511 % CPU through part of the run and a rustc
  build through another part (load average 2.7–7.9 across the session). Every
  sample is therefore `true_cost + contention` with contention >= 0, so the
  minimum is the only estimator that converges on the uncontended cost; a mean
  converges on the contention level, which is not a property of the code.
  Reported means come from zenbench's interleaved round-robin per cell, so
  ordering bias within a group is already removed — the min-of-runs handles
  the between-run level shifts contention causes.

measured contention sensitivity (why the single-thread table is the primary one):
  warm/oneshot RATIO, 4 runs, worst spread per cell —
    multi-thread : up to 58.6 % (zensim @1024²)
    single-thread: 3.8–13 % typical, and the ratio is FLAT across three orders
                   of magnitude of pixel count (butter 0.605–0.640,
                   zensim 0.883–0.938, ssim2 0.582–0.589)
  Absolute multi-thread wall on a contended box is unreliable; the ST table and
  the ratios are what the recommendation rests on. MT slopes are reported and
  used, with that caveat stated at every use.

zenbench resource gate: DISABLED (LOOP_WALL_NO_GATE=1). Its per-round `sysinfo`
  process enumeration costs more than the kernels being timed on macOS — with
  the gate on, a 64² zensim group ran 23.2 s at CV 138–205 %; with it off, 7.4 s
  with no CV warning and means ~40 % lower. Interleaved round-robin and paired
  statistics are unaffected; only the auto-pause is dropped. Same rationale and
  toggle as cpu-wall's CPU_WALL_NO_GATE (documented in
  cpu_wall_all_metrics_2026-05-29.meta).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORRECTNESS EVIDENCE (emitted in the same TSV as the wall time, by design —
# the recommendation is "drop the copy / keep the warm ref", which is only
# sound if the numbers do not move)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Every parity delta is EXACTLY 0.0 at every one of the 7 sizes:
  butter  strided-stride vs tight-stride score        : 0
  zensim  strided-stride vs tight-stride score        : 0
  zensim  repeated warm compare vs first warm compare : 0
  ssim2   warm-reference vs cold compute_ssimulacra2  : 0
  ssim2   compare_with(ctx) vs compare (fresh alloc)  : 0
Rows are in metric_loop_{st,mt}_2026-08-30.tsv with mode `PARITY:*`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FILES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
metric_loop_st_2026-08-30.tsv   min-of-4, single-thread (RAYON_NUM_THREADS=1)
metric_loop_mt_2026-08-30.tsv   min-of-4, multi-thread (crate defaults)
metric_loop_raw_2026-08-30.tsv.zst  all 10 CPU runs, every cell, unaggregated
metric_loop_gpu_metal_2026-08-30.tsv  Metal GPU: setup / cold / warm per
                                 candidate, both butteraugli memory modes,
                                 joined to this host's CPU for the crossover
metric_loop_2026-08-30.md       the decision + crossover rule

NO EXTRAPOLATION: every CPU cell is a real zenbench run at the stated dims on
this host, and every Metal GPU cell is a real run at the stated dims on this
host. No number in the writeup is scaled from another size or host. The CUDA
columns are quoted verbatim from the 2026-05-29 files and are labelled as such,
including the caveat that butteraugli-gpu's cached-reference behaviour CHANGED
after they were taken (commit 302ff4fc, 2026-05-31 — see the writeup). Metal and
CUDA are never combined into one figure.

GPU sample size: the Metal probe uses wall-clock `Instant` around each umbrella
call, 15 reps x 2 runs, min of per-run medians. That is thinner than the CPU
side's zenbench treatment and it is a HOST-SIDE timer — adequate here because
every call ends in a synchronous readback (`Score` is returned to the host), so
there is no un-awaited async submission to hide behind, but it is not a
kernel-level profile. The load-bearing GPU result is a RATIO measured inside one
process (`warm_ref / cold_warm`), which is insensitive to a constant timer bias;
the absolute setup figures should be re-taken with GPU events before being used
for anything finer than the order-of-magnitude crossover they support here.
